# Unending Grief!

We heard that Muthiah, the beloved son of our friend and "King of Music," Comrade Dandapani Desikar, died suddenly on 15 July 1944 after being ill for several days, and our hearts broke. The precious son of the king of music, who gave Tamils music and sweetness, had been a rare medicine to him.

Muthiah's little steps, smile, and childish speech were to him the yal and flute, melody and dance. With his heart bound in delight, joy dancing in his eyes, and happiness shining in his words, Desikar lived rejoicing in his dear son.

On 15 July 1944, as a flower garden is destroyed by a storm, as a lamp is extinguished by a great wind, as a vessel caught in a whirlpool strikes a rock and is shattered, and as a string of the yal snaps, Muthiah died and Desikar's mind fell into the ocean of inconsolable grief.

What can be said of this horror? With what words can Desikar's grief be soothed? However often one repeats "nature, nature," how can the sorrowful cry "My son! My son!" rising from his heart diminish? There is no grief more cruel than the loss of a child. Hearing that he has been plunged into it pains our hearts. Our own sorrow rises first and defeats our wish to console him. What can be done?
